---
import Image from "astro/components/Image.astro";
import Content from "../Content.astro";
import SectionHead from "../SectionHead.astro";
import Link from "../Link.astro";
import cntl from "cntl";
import lenomys from "../../assets/img/lenomys-meyeri.jpg";
import ProjectPubs from "../../layouts/ProjectPubs.astro";
import PageSubtitle from "../PageSubtitle.astro";
import LowSeaLevelMap from "./LowSeaLevelMap.astro";
import SkyIslandDiagram from "../svg/SkyIslandDiagram.astro";
import SulawesiAncestryMap from "./SulawesiAncestryMap.astro";
import ParasiteStudyDesign from "./ParasiteStudyDesign.astro";
import BunomysChronogram from "./BunomysChronogram.astro";
import QuestionIndex from "./QuestionIndex.astro";
import QuestionHead from "./QuestionHead.astro";
// import ElevationRanges from "./ElevationRanges.astro";

// The research questions drive both the index cards at the top and the
// heading of each section, so the two stay in sync.
const questions = [
  {
    id: "speciation-diversification",
    question: "How did the <i>Bunomys</i> clade diversify within a single island?",
    topic: "In situ diversification of the <i>Bunomys</i> clade on Sulawesi",
  },
  {
    id: "speciation-isolation",
    question: "Do mountains and areas of endemism keep populations apart?",
    topic: "Sulawesi as an island of islands",
  },
  {
    id: "speciation-parasites",
    question: "Do parasites follow their hosts' history or switch hosts?",
    topic: "Parasites: collaborations and next steps",
  },
];

const firstParagraphClasses = cntl`
  first-letter:text-7xl
  first-letter:font-semibold
  first-letter:mr-2
  first-letter:float-left
`;

const eyebrowClasses = cntl`
  mt-6
  text-xs
  font-semibold
  uppercase
  tracking-wider
  text-moss-700
  dark:text-moss-300
`;

const pubsLabelClasses = cntl`
  mt-6
  mb-1
  font-semibold
  text-moss-800
  dark:text-moss-200
`;
---

<div>
  <PageSubtitle
    subtitle="Systematics, Island Speciation, & Host-Parasite Interactions"
  />

  <QuestionIndex questions={questions} />

  <SectionHead
    text="Islands and mountains as natural laboratories of evolution"
  />
  <Content>
    <p class={firstParagraphClasses}>
      The Indo-Australian Archipelago (IAA) combines marine barriers, ancient
      paleo-islands, and steep mountains, a system where isolation has
      repeatedly driven diversification. During the Pleistocene, when sea levels
      were lower, Sumatra, Java, and Borneo joined mainland Asia as Sundaland,
      while Sulawesi, the Moluccas, and the Lesser Sundas stayed isolated by
      deep water. Like the Sunda Shelf, the Sahul Shelf was continuous landmass
      that connected the Australian continent to New Guinea during this time.
      Within islands, mountains act as sky islands. Many of these regions remain
      poorly surveyed. This exceptional geographic complexity provides a natural
      laboratory for disentangling how different forms and histories of
      isolation generate and maintain biodiversity. I ask how geographic
      isolation shapes genomic divergence, ecological interactions, and
      phenotypic evolution.
    </p>
  </Content>
  <p class={eyebrowClasses}>Pleistocene low sea-level boundaries</p>
  <div class="mt-2 rounded-2xl bg-white p-4 shadow-sm dark:bg-[#141810]">
    <LowSeaLevelMap />
  </div>

  <QuestionHead questions={questions} id="speciation-diversification" />
  <Content>
    <Image
      src={lenomys}
      alt="Lenomys meyeri, an endemic rat of Sulawesi"
      width={340}
      class="float-left mr-4 mt-2 rounded-xl"
    />
    <p>
      My main study system is the most speciose rodent clade on Sulawesi: the <i
        >Bunomys</i
      > clade. I study its systematics and in situ diversification. Integrating morphology,
      multi-marker Sanger sequencing, and ultra-conserved elements, we revealed taxonomic
      problems in the group and a complex interplay between paleo-islands, tectonic
      plates, and mountain formation in promoting its diversification, with mixed
      support for the Area of Endemism paradigm on Sulawesi.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      This work builds on 13 years of collaborative field expeditions across
      more than 20 remote mountain systems in Southeast Asia: over 20
      field-collecting expeditions contributing more than 8,000 voucher
      specimens and 30,000 specimen parts to natural history collections.
      Together with collaborators, this fieldwork has contributed to the
      description or revision of more than 24 mammal species. Beyond
      <i>Bunomys</i>, I support collaborative studies of shrew diversification
      on Sulawesi, Java, and Sumatra. I received the 2023 Murie Family
      Conservation Award from the American Society of Mammalogists for the
      significance of this work in conserving mammals and their habitats.
    </p>
  </Content>

  <p class={`clear-both ${eyebrowClasses}`}>
    Systematic and Divergence times of the Bunomys clade
  </p>
  <div class="mt-2 rounded-2xl bg-white p-4 shadow-sm dark:bg-[#141810]">
    <BunomysChronogram />
  </div>

  <Content>
    <p class={pubsLabelClasses}>Publications:</p>
    <ProjectPubs ids={["frateromys", "handika2021"]} />
    <ProjectPubs ids={["esselstyn2021", "esselstyn2019", "esselstyn2015"]} />
    <ProjectPubs ids={["giarla2025"]} />
  </Content>

  <QuestionHead questions={questions} id="speciation-isolation" />
  <Content>
    <p>
      Sulawesi is a complex island with a rich geological history. It has been
      shaped by ancient paleo-islands, tectonic plate movements, and mountain
      formation. Together, these factors have shaped the <i>Bunomys</i> radiation.
      Above about 1,000 m, many species are confined to a single mountain, while others
      have widespread distributions that span multiple mountains and across the island.
    </p>
  </Content>
  <p class={eyebrowClasses}>Four sky islands in the central core</p>
  <div class="mt-2 rounded-2xl bg-white p-4 shadow-sm dark:bg-[#141810]">
    <SkyIslandDiagram />
  </div>

  {
    /* Elevational distribution plot hidden for now.
  <p class={eyebrowClasses}>Across elevations</p>
  <Content>
    <p>
      Across the whole clade the pattern holds. More than half of the
      species and haplogroups we sampled are montane, most known from a single
      mountain. Widespread taxa cross the 1,000 m threshold, and a few, such
      as <i>T. dominator</i> and <i>B. penitus</i> WC-A, span nearly the whole
      gradient.
      Filter by range class or area of endemism to explore them.
    </p>
  </Content>
  <div class="mt-4 rounded-2xl bg-white p-4 shadow-sm dark:bg-[#141810]">
    <ElevationRanges />
  </div>
  */
  }

  <p class={eyebrowClasses}>Across the island</p>
  <Content>
    <p>
      Sulawesi is often divided into seven areas of endemism (AoEs) separated by
      narrow boundary zones. <i>Taeromys dominator</i>, a large endemic rat
      found across the island and from lowlands to high elevations, shows how
      messy the system is: one ancestry cluster spans mountains in every area of
      endemism, populations in the southeast carry a distinct cluster, and
      several individuals are admixed, so the AoE boundaries are only partly
      respected.
    </p>
  </Content>
  <div class="mt-4 rounded-2xl bg-white p-4 shadow-sm dark:bg-[#141810]">
    <SulawesiAncestryMap />
    <p class="mt-4 text-pretty text-sm text-forest-700 dark:text-forest-300">
      Ancestry of 29 <i>T. dominator</i> from 12 localities, estimated from ultra-conserved
      element (UCE) SNPs with PopCluster at K = 4, the best-supported K by DLK2. Each
      pie is one individual; individuals from the same locality are fanned out around
      it. The bands mark the boundary zones between areas of endemism.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Content>
    <p class={pubsLabelClasses}>Publications:</p>
    <ProjectPubs ids={["bunomys-phylogenomics"]} />
    <ProjectPubs ids={["chipps-crocidura"]} />
  </Content>

  <QuestionHead questions={questions} id="speciation-parasites" />
  <Content>
    <p>
      The evolutionary history of the <i>Bunomys</i> clade also provides a framework
      for asking whether parasites co-evolve with their hosts or cross species boundaries.
      Work led by <Link
        text="Kevin Rowe's lab at Museums Victoria"
        url="https://museumsvictoria.com.au/about-us/board-and-staff/staff/dr-kevin-rowe/"
      />, Australia, on which I am a co-author, documented native and introduced <i
        >Trypanosoma</i
      > lineages in Sulawesi rodents, with high prevalence in several <i
        >Bunomys</i
      >
      species and infections from village edges to remote montane forests, peaking
      above 2,000 m.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-2">
      Building on these collaborations, parasite evolution is a direction I hope
      to pursue by integrating host population genomics, parasite phylogenies,
      environmental data, and single-cell sequencing. This framework would test
      whether parasite diversification parallels host history or reflects host
      switching and local adaptation, while also examining how these processes
      shape host-cell tropism and immune responses. Single-cell or
      single-nucleus sequencing of infected tissues could link host and parasite
      genetic variation to the specific cell types and transcriptional states
      associated with infection. To lay the groundwork, <Link
        text="NAHPU"
        url="https://nahpu.app"
      /> records parasites collected in the field alongside their host specimens and
      tracks their screening, keeping parasite identity, infection status, and molecular
      data linked to the host from which they originated.
    </p>
  </Content>
  <ParasiteStudyDesign />

  <Content>
    <p class={pubsLabelClasses}>Publications:</p>
    <ProjectPubs ids={["adams2025"]} />
    <ProjectPubs ids={["mursyid2023"]} />
    <ProjectPubs ids={["winterhoff2020"]} />
  </Content>

  <Content>
    <p class="mt-12 mb-1 font-semibold text-moss-800 dark:text-moss-200">
      Other publications:
    </p>
    <ProjectPubs ids={["demos2016", "demos2017"]} />
    <ProjectPubs ids={["swanson2023"]} />
    <ProjectPubs ids={["nations2024-shrews"]} />
  </Content>
</div>
<div
  class="absolute inset-x-0 top-[calc(100%-13rem)] -z-10 transform-gpu overflow-hidden blur-3xl sm:top-[calc(100%-30rem)]"
  aria-hidden="true"
>
  <br class="" />
</div>
